<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 12 and later. What makes this restrictive is the single <include>: the
  moment a section contains any include at all, ONLY those paths travel.
  Everything the old default was carrying — user.db, the photographs of packs,
  the WebView's own app_webview directory, and the 39 MB copy of usda_core.db
  that db::resolve puts in the app data directory — is excluded by construction
  rather than by a list somebody has to remember to extend.

  domain="file" is getFilesDir(). Note that Tauri's app_data_dir() is NOT that:
  on Android it resolves to activity.dataDir, one level up. That is why the
  sealed file is written to the path BackupPlugin.backupDir() reports and
  nowhere else, so the path named here is exact rather than inferred from a
  relationship between two directories that nothing documents. The key
  material, which lives under app_data_dir()/keys, is therefore not merely
  excluded — there is no path in this file that could name it.

  disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities is deliberately absent from <cloud-backup>.
  It would silently switch the whole backup off on a phone with no screen lock,
  which is precisely the kind of quiet nothing-happened this feature exists to
  avoid. It is also unnecessary: the file is already sealed with the user's own
  passphrase before Google ever sees it.

  <device-transfer> carries the same one file. Phone-to-phone transfer is local
  and has no quota, so it could have carried the photographs and the database
  whole — that is given up so there is exactly one true sentence to tell the
  user about what leaves this phone.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
  <cloud-backup>
    <include domain="file" path="backup/trackit-backup.tkb" />
  </cloud-backup>
  <device-transfer>
    <include domain="file" path="backup/trackit-backup.tkb" />
  </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
